  "notes": [
    "Scope: Worldwide merchant DRAM (including FPM/EDO, SDRAM, DDR1–DDR5, LPDDR1–LPDDR5X, GDDR, and High Bandwidth Memory / HBM) and standalone (discrete) SRAM ICs across 1980–2026 (47 years). Embedded SRAM (on-die CPU/GPU/SoC/ASIC L1/L2/L3 cache macros) is NOT counted in standalone SRAM figures.",
    "Unit identity & reconciliation: Capacity is in decimal exabytes (1 EB = 10^18 bytes = 10^9 GB; 1 Gb = 0.125 GB; 8 billion Gb = 1 EB) and revenue is in nominal US$ billions (USD_B), so price ($/GB) = revenue (USD_B) / capacity (EB). Across all 47 years for both DRAM and SRAM, |price - revenue/capacity| / price is < 2.5%, and all values are rounded to 2–3 significant digits.",
    "DRAM historical cycles (1980–2017): Captures the 1984 PC boom ($3.5B) and 1985 dumping crash ($1.7B, Intel exits DRAM), the 1988–1989 FMV trade-agreement shortage ($7.8B), the 1995 Windows 95 / 4Mb–16Mb boom peak ($40.8B) and 1996–1998 slump ($14.0B in 1998, TI exits to Micron), the 2000 dot-com peak ($28.9B) and 2001 crash ($11.2B, -61%), the 2008–2009 GFC downturn ($22.7B, Qimonda insolvency), and the 2012 Elpida bankruptcy ($26.3B) that consolidated the industry into the 'Big Three' oligopoly (Samsung, SK hynix, Micron).",
    "DRAM supercycles & AI/HBM era (2018–2026E): Reflects the 2018 cloud/mobile supercycle peak ($99.4B, 14.6 EB at $6.81/GB), 2019 correction ($62.2B), 2021 pandemic peak ($95.2B, 22.6 EB), 2023 cyclical trough ($52.0B, 26.5 EB at $1.96/GB), 2024 AI rebound ($90.7B, 31.3 EB / 250B Gb per TrendForce and Frost & Sullivan), 2025 expansion ($137.0B, 37.5 EB / 300B Gb comprising $105.0B / 34.3 EB conventional DRAM + $32.0B / 3.2 EB HBM), and 2026E AI HBM3e/HBM4 allocation supercycle ($195.0B, 43.5 EB at $4.48/GB).",
    "Standalone SRAM revenue trajectory: Standalone SRAM grew from ~$0.38B in 1980 to a peak of $4.8B in 1995 (driven by external 386/486/Pentium motherboard L2 cache chips) and $4.5B in 2000 (telecom/networking switches and 2G handset SRAM). Once CPUs integrated L2/L3 cache on-die and mobile phones migrated to LPDDR, standalone SRAM contracted from $2.2B in 2005 and $1.6B in 2010 (IC Insights) to ~$0.39B–$0.48B in 2017–2026E (IC Insights $406M in 2018, $394M in 2019; TheLec ~$400M in 2023).",
    "Standalone SRAM capacity & pricing method (flagged 'E'/'I'): Because SRAM uses a 6-transistor (6T) cell occupying 6x–12x the die area of a 1T1C DRAM cell, standalone SRAM commanded a ~6x–10x per-bit price premium over DRAM during the high-volume 1980–2000 cache era ($4.8M/GB in 1980 -> $80,000/GB in 1995 -> $5,000/GB in 2000). Post-2000, as standalone SRAM specialized into low-density (1Mb–144Mb), ultra-low-latency networking (QDR-II+/IV, SyncBurst) and high-reliability industrial/automotive/aerospace chips on mature nodes, blended ASP plateaued around $1,140–$1,450/GB (~$1.14–$1.45/MB). Capacity (EB) is derived via revenue(USD_B) / price($/GB), yielding ~0.00034–0.00040 EB/yr (340–400 TB/yr, or ~250M chips/yr at ~12Mb average density) in 2017–2026E."
